NNAAMMEE
       ppmflash - brighten a picture to approach white


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppmflash _f_l_a_s_h_f_a_c_t_o_r [_p_p_m_f_i_l_e]


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppppmmffllaasshh  reads  a  PPM  image  as input.  It changes the color of each
       pixel to bring it a specified amount closer to white.  It  generates  a
       PPM image of the result.

       _f_l_a_s_h_f_a_c_t_o_r  is a real number between 0 and 1, inclusive.  ppppmmffllaasshh in‐
       creases the intensity of each RGB component by the fraction _f_l_a_s_h_f_a_c_t_o_r
       of  the difference between the current value and full intensity.  So if
       a pixel contains 60% full red, 10% full green,  and  no  blue  and  you
       specify  0.5  (half), ppppmmffllaasshh increases the red to 80% (because it was
       40% from full intensity, so it adds half of 40% to the  original  60%),
       the green to 55%, and the blue to 50%.

       If  _f_l_a_s_h_f_a_c_t_o_r  is  zero,  the  output  is identical to the input.  If
       _f_l_a_s_h_f_a_c_t_o_r is one, the output is all white.

       ppaammbbrriigghhtteenn does a more normal kind of  brightening.   ppaammffuunncc  does  a
       very  simple  brightening.   Both  ppaammbbrriigghhtteenn  and  ppaammffuunncc can reduce
       brightness as well.

       ppnnmmggaammmmaa is another way people do  a  similar  brightening,  though  it
       isn't really intended for that.


